<p>The design choice is notable: in ordinary civil and criminal trials, closed hearings are exceptional and justified case by case; in matrimonial proceedings the legislature pre-made the judgment, leaving the switch in the hands of the parties themselves.</p>

<h2>What a closed hearing changes — and what it does not</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>Changed</h4><p>The public and press leave the courtroom; testimony on intimate allegations is taken before the judge, counsel and parties alone; the gallery pressure that distorts cross-examination on personal matters is removed.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>Unchanged</h4><p>The record: pleadings, depositions and exhibits remain on the file, open to the parties, the appellate court and persons the court permits. In camera is privacy of audience, not secrecy of record.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The publication bar and the social-media age</h2>
<p>Section 22 HMA pairs the closed hearing with a restraint that today matters more than the courtroom door: it is unlawful to print or publish any matter in relation to such proceedings, except a judgment of a High Court or of the Supreme Court printed or published with the previous permission of the court, and contravention is punishable with fine. The provision was drafted for newspapers, but its language comfortably covers the modern equivalents — posting pleadings on social media, circulating recorded testimony, publicising allegations from a pending petition. Parties engaged in online campaigns about their matrimonial case expose themselves to the penalty, to injunctions, and to an adverse colouring of their conduct in the pending proceedings.</p>

<h2>Using the regime well</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ask early and on record.</strong> A written request at the first effective hearing, or before evidence begins, puts the mandatory language of Section 22(1) HMA or Section 11 FCA to work; renew it when the matter travels to a new court.</li>
    <li><strong>Pair it with anonymisation where needed.</strong> In sensitive cases parties may seek that cause lists and the judgment carry initials; appellate courts routinely accommodate this.</li>
    <li><strong>Mind connected proceedings.</strong> A DV complaint, a maintenance petition and a divorce case between the same spouses may run in different courts; the privacy request must be made in each, under the provision each forum administers.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not weaponise privacy.</strong> The closed hearing protects testimony; it does not license suppressing documents from the other side or resisting lawful disclosure — the opponent and the court see everything.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>For litigants, the message is reassuring: the price of seeking a divorce, maintenance or custody is not the surrender of one\'s private life to the public record. The statute keeps the courtroom door closed on request — and expects the parties to keep their own channels closed in return.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Procedural practice varies between courts, and the applicable provision depends on the forum and the statute invoked.</p></div>
